# Kernel: dab
#
# One stamp of the brush tip, evaluated analytically at the size and rotation
# the dab asks for. No dab cache and no tip atlas: a cached mask is rasterised at
# one size and resampled to every other.
#
# Every function returns coverage, 1 for opaque and 0 for clear, the sense a
# painter states a mask in and the sense the alpha rules want. The other way
# round would put a subtraction at nine call sites.
# The falloffs and the sharpness cut live in the `brush_tip` library, the
# uniforms and the push constant here, and the per-texel steps in `dab_texel`,
# which `dabs.gmacs` runs with the same lines.
#
# Written from what the mechanisms do, so this file carries no copyright line but
# Sara's own. The one port, the alpha arithmetic of three texture modes, is in
# `pattern_modes.gmacs` with its lines in `PROVENANCE.md`.
#
# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later

kernel dab

import noise
# How the dab's paint lands on the texel in each of the three paint modes, the
# door's library.
import brush_paint_modes
# The deposition rule, which the eraser lowers the paint's height by.
import height_deposit
# For the tip's lightness map: the dab's colour is decoded out
# of its sRGB curve, moved in Oklab's lightness and encoded back, which is the
# maths `SaColorDynamics` already does on the CPU for Hue, Saturation and Value
# and the same pair of libraries it does it with. Imported from here rather than
# from `brush_tip`, the falloffs' library, because that file is text pasted
# after these uniforms and would carry the import past them, and because a
# falloff has no colour in it.
import linear_srgb_color_space
# For the four numbers an RGB-storing document's values were encoded through.
# The curve is the document's own rather than the sRGB one: a gamma 2.2 document
# would otherwise have its lightness shifted under the wrong curve, which is a
# near miss on a mid grey and a long way off on a shadow.
import transfer_curve

image canvas
# The document-wide selection, r32f whatever the canvas is, on the same chunk
# grid, bound for every target by `SaPaintContext.dispatch_batch`. A 1 by 1
# mask is the no-selection stand-in and the clamped read is what makes it 1.0
# everywhere without a flag (core/api/selection/sa_selection.gd).
image mask: r32f, readonly
# The SOFT generator's falloff, `SaBrushTip.SOFTNESS_SAMPLES` floats of
# coverage against the squared normalised radius. Bound for every dispatch,
# whichever generator is running, because a uniform set has to cover every
# binding the kernel declares and a table nothing reads costs one bind.
#
# Binding 2, with the image tip on 3. A declared binding with nothing in it is a
# broken uniform set rather than a spare slot, so a table holds the number it
# was given and no slot is kept free for a table to come.
buffer softness: float curve[]
# The image tip's coverage, at the file's own resolution, sampled bilinearly at
# the dab's own inverse transform. **One texture serves every size**, so
# there is no mipmap pyramid and no per-size cache: the kernel asks for a
# normalised coordinate and the sampler does the rest.
#
# Binding 3, the softness table holding 2, because a binding arrives with the
# code that reads it and no number is kept free for a reader to come. A one by
# one white texel is bound when the tip is procedural, which is the pattern
# `SaSelection.everything()` established and which the rule about a uniform set
# covering every declared binding makes necessary rather than tidy.
sampler tip_image
# The tiled mask the paper is painted through, read by `sa_pattern_applied`
# below whenever `pattern_strength` is above zero. Still bound to the same one
# by one stand-in when it is not, because a declared binding with no uniform is
# a set that refuses to build, and the stand-in is clear rather than white,
# which is why nothing must read it at strength zero.
sampler pattern
# The masking brush's second tip: its own numbers first and its
# own softness table behind them, in one storage buffer.
#
# **Not the push constant, because there is none left.** The pattern filled that
# block to its 128 bytes exactly, and a second tip needs eleven numbers rather
# than the one a pattern needed, and a storage buffer is where side data goes
# once the block is closed.
#
# **Not a second dispatch either.** A dab is one pass over its own rectangle and
# a mask is a multiply on the coverage that pass computes, so a second dispatch
# would have to keep the first one's coverage somewhere to multiply it by, which
# is a full-size scratch texture per target and a read of it, for arithmetic
# that costs eleven reads out of a buffer here.
#
# **Bound on every dispatch, whether or not there is a mask**, because a uniform
# set has to cover every binding the kernel declares. The stand-in is the same
# array with its first float at nought, which `sa_mask_applied` answers on
# before it reads anything else.
#
# **The painted tip's lightness row rides here too**, at
# `SA_MASK_TIP_LIGHTNESS`, for the reason the block above is full: the number
# belongs to the painted tip rather than to the mask, and this is the only
# buffer the kernel has that is not the push constant. Its header is twenty
# floats rather than thirteen, the mask's own numbers plus the painted tip's
# lightness row, its mode, the four transfer numbers and
# the mask's composite op at 19, and the softness table behind it moves with
# it. A slot added here is a header one longer and nothing else, because the
# table is found at `SA_MASK_HEADER` and not at a number of its own.
#
# **The mask's scatter makes it twenty-two**, as two numbers behind
# the op. The slots go on the end and the softness table moves behind them, so
# no slot anything already read changes its index. The mask's rotation needs
# no slot, because it turns the cosine and sine the mask already carries.
# The document's two gamut matrices behind it, at 22 and 31, make it forty.
buffer masking: float second_tip[]
# The smudge stage's carry, written once per dab by `smudge_pickup` before this
# kernel runs. Texel 0 is the carried colour and, in alpha, how much of it
# replaces the brush's own colour on this dab. The stage off binds
# `SaSmudgeReservoir.stand_in()`, one texel of nothing, and an alpha of nought
# mixes nothing in, so a dab with the stage off paints exactly the texels it
# would with no stage at all. Binding 6, and bound on every dispatch for the
# reason the two buffers above give.
image smudge: rgba32f, readonly
# The paint's height under the dab, which only the erase mode
# writes: erasing lowers the paint rather than taking its colour alone. The
# layer's own chunk height where the stroke erases straight into a layer that
# carries one, and `SaStrokeHeights.held`'s one texel stand-in everywhere else,
# which the kernel knows by its size and never writes. Binding 7, bound on
# every dispatch for the reason the buffers above give. A smudge stroke that
# moves height writes it too, which binds the layer's height
# through `SaStrokeHeights.reach` rather than `held`.
image height: r16f
# What a smudging dab found under itself, for the pickup of the next dab to
# carry: the sum of the coverage and of the coverage times the
# height read before the write, in fixed point, which `smudge_pickup.gmacs`
# reads and clears. `SaSmudgeReservoir.bind` puts the carry's own buffer here
# or a stand-in, since only a stroke that moves height adds to it. Binding 8.
buffer exchange: uint sums[]

# The preset's ramp a gradient-map tip paints through, as the document's own
# values at the centre of each texel, which `SaStampBindings.Ramp` uploads
# before a stroke opens and never inside one. Its own binding, because the block
# below is full, and the one by one stand-in when the mode is off, which is what
# `sa_ramp_color` tests. Binding 9.
sampler ramp

# The full 128 bytes a push constant may carry, so what comes later adds a field
# rather than a layout. Everything past `randomness` is written at the value
# that means "not asked for", and each is named here so the next reader knows
# the slot is taken rather than free.
params:
    vec2 dab_center  # chunk-local document pixels, sub-pixel
    vec2 render_offset  # the dispatch origin in the chunk's own texels
    vec4 color  # the document's encoding, alpha is the dab opacity
    vec2 half_extent  # major and minor radii in pixels
    vec2 rotation  # cos and sin of the dab angle, computed on the CPU
    vec2 fade  # horizontal and vertical, already multiplied by softness
    float flow
    float spikes  # 2 is the plain ellipse and the only value that skips the fold.
    float generator  # 0 default, 1 gauss, 2 soft curve, 3 image.
    float shape  # 0 circle, 1 rectangle.
    float antialias
    float density
    float randomness
    float seed  # Per dab, from the dab sequence number.
    float sharpness
    float sharpness_soft
    float paint_mode  # 0 over, 1 alpha darken, 2 erase.
    float alpha_lock
    float mirror  # Bit 0 horizontal, bit 1 vertical.
    # The hardness ellipse an image tip is softened along, in pixels, computed
    # on the CPU by `SaDabGeometry.falloff_radius`. Two floats
    # rather than a `vec2`, because std430 would align a vec2 to eight bytes
    # and this pair sits at offset 108.
    float falloff_x
    float falloff_y
    # The pattern hook beside binding 4, filled in by the pattern.
    # `pattern_strength` is how much of the tiled mask the dab's coverage is
    # given over to, and zero is the dab with no pattern at all, arithmetically
    # as well as by the early return below. Its sign is the invert toggle and
    # its band is the texturing mode, both read in `sa_pattern_applied`: a
    # magnitude at or under one is multiply and one above is subtract, with the
    # strength one less. Two things ride one float because the block below is
    # full and no unit widens it.
    #
    # `pattern_offset` is the document texel the tile grid starts at, and it
    # carries the chunk's own document origin as well as whatever the painter
    # asked for: `document_pixel` below is chunk-local, so a grid stated in
    # chunk coordinates would break at every chunk edge a tile size does not
    # divide. Two floats rather than a `vec2` for the reason the pair above
    # gives, and the three of them fill the block to its 128 bytes exactly, so
    # there is no pad at the tail and nothing spare.
    float pattern_strength
    float pattern_offset_x
    float pattern_offset_y

# One texel of the dab, the lines `dabs.gmacs` runs for each dab of a walk as
# well, which is why they are a file of their own.
import dab_texel


def dab():
    # `pixel_coords` is the texel this invocation writes, and `render_offset`
    # is in the canvas's texels, so the dispatch covers the dab's own box.
    ivec2 pixel_coords = ivec2(global_id.xy) + ivec2(render_offset)
    ivec2 size = image_size(canvas)
    if pixel_coords.x >= size.x or pixel_coords.y >= size.y:
        return
    sa_dab_texel(pixel_coords)
